### What are raw types?

A raw type is a generic class or interface used without specifying type arguments. Before Java 5, all collections were raw: `List`, `Map`, `Set`. Raw types still compile in modern Java for backward compatibility, but they bypass all generic type checking.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class RawTypeDemo {
    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Raw type: no type safety
        List rawList = new ArrayList();
        rawList.add("SAV2001");
        rawList.add(42);        // no error! compiler does not check
        rawList.add(3.14);      // no error!

        System.out.println("Raw list: " + rawList);
        System.out.println("Size: " + rawList.size());

        // Parameterised type: compile-time safety
        List<String> typedList = new ArrayList<>();
        typedList.add("SAV2001");
        // typedList.add(42);   // COMPILE ERROR
    }
}
```

```text
Raw list: [SAV2001, 42, 3.14]
Size: 3
```

### What is heap pollution?

Heap pollution occurs when a variable of a parameterised type refers to an object that is not of that type. This typically happens when raw types are mixed with generic types.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class HeapPollutionDemo {
    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> accounts = new ArrayList<>();
        accounts.add("SAV2001");

        // Raw reference bypasses generics
        List rawRef = accounts;
        rawRef.add(42);         // Integer sneaks into List<String>!
        rawRef.add(new Object()); // Object sneaks in too!

        System.out.println("Size: " + accounts.size());

        // The ClassCastException occurs HERE, far from the insertion
        String first = accounts.get(0);  // OK
        System.out.println("First: " + first);

        try {
            String second = accounts.get(1); // ClassCastException!
        } catch (ClassCastException e) {
            System.out.println("Heap pollution caught at get(1): " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Size: 3
First: SAV2001
Heap pollution caught at get(1): class java.lang.Integer cannot be cast to class java.lang.String
```

### Why is heap pollution dangerous?

The ClassCastException from heap pollution occurs at the point of retrieval, not at the point of insertion. The stack trace points to the get() call, which might be in a completely different method, class, or even module from where the wrong type was inserted. This makes the bug extremely hard to trace.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class DelayedCCEDemo {

    // This method inserts the wrong type (the bug is HERE)
    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
    static void legacyPopulate(List list) {
        list.add("SAV2001");
        list.add(99999);  // Integer inserted into what will be List<String>
    }

    // This method reads the list (the CCE happens HERE)
    static void processAccounts(List<String> accounts) {
        for (String account : accounts) {
            System.out.println("Processing: " + account);
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> accounts = new ArrayList<>();
        legacyPopulate(accounts); // bug introduced here

        try {
            processAccounts(accounts); // CCE happens here, far from the bug
        } catch (ClassCastException e) {
            System.out.println("CCE in processAccounts: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Processing: SAV2001
CCE in processAccounts: class java.lang.Integer cannot be cast to class java.lang.String
```

### What is @SafeVarargs?

When a method has generic varargs (`T... args`), the compiler creates an `Object[]` at runtime because of type erasure. This creates a potential for heap pollution if the method stores into the varargs array. The @SafeVarargs annotation tells the compiler that the method does not store into the array, suppressing the warning.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;

public class SafeVarargsDemo {

    // SAFE: only reads from the varargs array
    @SafeVarargs
    static <T> List<T> toList(T... elements) {
        return Arrays.asList(elements);
    }

    // UNSAFE: stores into the varargs array
    static <T> void unsafeStore(T... elements) {
        Object[] arr = elements; // varargs is Object[] at runtime
        arr[0] = 42;            // stores Integer, might corrupt T[]
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> accounts = toList("SAV2001", "CUR3001", "FD4001");
        System.out.println("Safe varargs: " + accounts);

        try {
            unsafeStore("SAV2001", "CUR3001");
        } catch (ArrayStoreException e) {
            System.out.println("Unsafe store: " + e.getClass().getSimpleName());
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Safe varargs: [SAV2001, CUR3001, FD4001]
Unsafe store: ArrayStoreException
```

### What are common mistakes?

#### Mistake: Using raw types in new code

```java
// WRONG: raw type, unchecked
Map map = new HashMap();
map.put("SAV2001", "Rajesh");
map.put(42, 3.14); // no error, corrupted map
```

```java
// CORRECT: parameterised type
Map<String, String> map = new HashMap<>();
map.put("SAV2001", "Rajesh");
// map.put(42, 3.14); // COMPILE ERROR
```

#### Mistake: Using @SafeVarargs on an unsafe method

```java
// WRONG: method stores into varargs array
@SafeVarargs // LYING to the compiler!
static <T> void dangerous(T... items) {
    Object[] array = items;
    array[0] = new Object(); // heap pollution!
}
```

```java
// CORRECT: @SafeVarargs only on methods that READ varargs
@SafeVarargs
static <T> List<T> safe(T... items) {
    return List.of(items); // only reads, never stores
}
```

### How can you detect heap pollution early?

Use `Collections.checkedList` to catch the wrong type at insertion time instead of retrieval time.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.List;

public class CheckedListDemo {
    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> checked = Collections.checkedList(new ArrayList<>(), String.class);
        List rawRef = checked;

        try {
            rawRef.add(42); // ClassCastException HERE, at insertion!
        } catch (ClassCastException e) {
            System.out.println("Caught at add: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Caught at add: Attempt to insert class java.lang.Integer element into collection with element type class java.lang.String
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **unchecked warning is meaningful.** Every unchecked warning is a potential heap pollution. Treat them seriously; do not blindly suppress them.
- **@SafeVarargs restrictions.** The annotation can only be placed on static methods, final methods, constructors, and private methods (since Java 9), because the compiler must ensure the method cannot be overridden with an unsafe implementation.
- **Legacy migration strategy.** When migrating from raw types to generics, use Collections.checkedList/Map/Set at API boundaries to catch pollution from legacy code.
- **SonarQube and IDE warnings.** Modern IDEs and static analysis tools flag raw type usage and unchecked casts. Configure them as errors, not warnings.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "What is heap pollution?" : A variable of a generic type holds a reference to an object of a different type, causing ClassCastException at retrieval.
- "When does the ClassCastException occur with heap pollution?" : At retrieval time (get), not at insertion time, making it hard to debug.
- "What does @SafeVarargs promise?" : That the method does not store into the varargs array, so no heap pollution can occur through varargs.
- "How do you prevent heap pollution?" : Never use raw types, never suppress unchecked warnings without verification, and use Collections.checkedList at legacy boundaries.
